   "brief": [
    "The owner decided Q1 (Zulip #Tarn > kitchen, 2393, option C). Today the build's request id hashes the cook-adapter argv, i.e. the paths of Node and `dist/cook-adapter.js` (review L2: src/engine.ts:68-70, src/deploy.ts:582). That means every Node upgrade or move of kitchen rebuilds and restarts every agent. With C, kitchen's request identity uses a named, versioned adapter contract instead of paths, e.g. `kitchen-cook-adapter/1`. The number moves only when what the adapter puts into a build changes. The command actually executed stays the real one. Kitchen clone `.cache/kitchen/repo` is at 15ee4a1.",
    "This is a deliberate divergence from the oracle (which hashes `sys.executable` plus the script path). Record it as a named ruling, and make the corpus compare it on purpose rather than hide it: the oracle side keeps its own id, and the TS side must be shown to be identical in everything except the identity words. Add a guard so the version can't silently go stale: a test where the adapter's observable output (lock post-processing, modes, staged files) is pinned to the contract version, so any change in that output fails until the version is bumped.",
    "Say what it means for taking over each of us: the first kitchen apply is a new build plus a restart, once, and none after for Node or kitchen moves. Show that in the corpus (same inputs, different Node path or install location: same id). `npm test` and the corpus on both Nodes. No cook, no accounts. Commit, write report.md as you go, and finalize."
   ],
