   "as_received": "Goal: build F4 as declared divergence D4. Use controlled admission budgets, checked before the allocation they bound, so that the port answers with a stable typed refusal instead of a heap abort. Your proposed numbers are only starting candidates. Soma (technical owner) wants them calibrated from evidence, not asserted.",
   "as_received_points_of_the_technical_owner": [
    "1. Byte accounting. Unknown pax values may stay out of the byte budget only if you show they are stream-skipped: no large decode, buffer, or backing string retained through a slice. Otherwise count them, or prove a separate transient bound. Counting all pax metadata bytes read is an acceptable, conservative D4 contract. Choose one and justify it. Don't cover the gap with the record counter.",
    "2. Upper bound. \">40 bytes/number at an abort\" is a lower estimate, not a basis for a guarantee. Measure completed sparse and member costs on both Node versions, plus combined cases with several budgets near their limits at once, and derive the bound from those measurements. \"Packages don't need sparse\" doesn't replace a contract.",
    "3. Sparse units. GNU 1.0's declared count is pairs (regions), and your budget is in numbers. Check 2xcount without overflow. Give 0.0, 0.1, 1.0 and old GNU the same semantics, with no hidden factor of two.",
    "4. Heap precondition. A 2 GiB heap_size_limit is a candidate too, not a RAM guarantee. It doesn't reserve physical or cgroup memory, doesn't cover Buffer and native allocations, and says nothing about the heap being empty. State an honest runtime contract with measured headroom, and check it fits the target CLI. The role must not silently raise the heap to make a gate green."
   ],
   "as_received_from_the_delta_review": [
    "P0: make the byte budget the size field of every L/K/x/g/X header read, cumulatively. D3 becomes its single-block case, and this settles Soma's point 1 conservatively unless you prove stream-skip.",
