     "heap limit 2,048 MiB: the brief of the closing step (point 3) asks for a requirement of at most 2,048 MiB that Node's default heap meets in a unit of 4 GiB or more, measured at exactly that limit with the review's wide global map included. The implementer measured it (`measured_at_that_limit`)."
    ],
    "what_the_heap_number_is": "A bound on `v8.getHeapStatistics().heap_size_limit`, which is the old space `--max-old-space-size` sets plus the young generation. It is not the flag: on the development host a limit of exactly 2,048 MiB is `--max-old-space-size=2000` on Node 22.23.3 and `=1856` on 24.14.0. The reader checks the limit itself, at run time. The measurements ran at a limit of exactly 2,048 MiB.",
    "measured_at_that_limit": "The costliest archive found within the budgets (test/differential/port_budget.py, combined.heap-worst: members, sparse numbers and metadata bytes one under their budgets; member names and one pax path that are no UTF-8; a sparse map of 15 numbers of 4,300 digits in a global header, expanded 4,369 times, which is the shape of the delta review's LOW-2). Node 22.23.3 / 24.14.0, heap in use, sampled inside the process. One listing: 321 / 341 MiB at the peak, 282 MiB kept, 46 / 78 MiB of Buffers. Three listings one after another with the listing before still referenced, which is more than archive_manifest holds between its calls: 801 / 631 MiB at the peak (606 to 801 across runs on 22.23.3). The same with 536 MiB of caller data on the heap: 1,337 / 1,168 MiB. With 1,076 MiB of caller data: 1,799 / 1,720 MiB in use, 1,847 / 1,804 MiB committed, inside the limit, completes on both. With 1,344 MiB: completes on 22.23.3 at 1,931 MiB, aborts on 24.14.0. Through archive_manifest itself, three calls in a row: 387 / 389 MiB in use afterwards with no caller data, 1,437 MiB with 1,056 MiB of caller data; one call with 1,332 / 1,324 MiB of caller data completes on both. Resident memory of the port on that archive in the gate: about 500 MiB.",
    "margin": "1,247 MiB between the highest peak of consecutive listings (801 MiB) and the limit: 61% of the heap. The margin is measured on the costliest archive found, not proven for every archive within the budgets.",
    "default_heap_of_a_unit": "With no heap flag, inside a systemd scope with the given MemoryMax and no swap, archive_manifest on that archive, three calls: under 4G, 6G and 8G the default limit is 2,096 MiB on Node 22.23.3 and 2,240 MiB on 24.14.0, and the archive is read. Under 2G the default is 1,048 / 1,120 MiB and every call ends in UnsupportedRuntime; such a unit needs `--max-old-space-size=2048` on the command line of Node (backlog B14). Not chosen: a requirement of 1,024 MiB, which a unit of 2 GiB would meet. Measured for information at exactly that limit, one listing completes with 536 MiB of caller data, and three with the listing before still referenced abort on Node 24.14.0 with 269 MiB of caller data.",
    "environment_requirement": "A V8 heap limit of at least 2,048 MiB, which Node's default gives in a unit of 4 GiB of memory or more, and memory for what the process comes to use: for the costliest archive found about 0.5 GiB resident, plus what the caller holds. No unit that runs kitchen exists yet; this is a requirement on the environment, and the role was not changed.",
    "evidence": "docs/port-notes.md, \"The admission budgets\"; test/vectors/port-budget.golden; the report of the closing step with its logs (calib-heap-worst-at-2048.log, manifest-heap-worst-at-2048.log, default-heap-in-units.log, calib-heap-worst-at-1024-informational.log)."
   },
   "choices": [
    "Byte accounting (point 1, P0): every metadata byte read is counted, kept or not. The unit is the size field of every L, K, x, g and X header whose blocks are read, plus 512 for every block read for a sparse map (the data blocks of form 1.0, old GNU extension blocks). The second part goes beyond P0: the numbers of form 1.0 are member data, up to about a thousand digits each, and no header's size field covers them.",
    "Sparse unit (point 3): numbers, in all four forms; a region is two. The count a 1.0 map states is a count of regions and is never compared with the budget: each number is counted as it is read, so 2 x count is not computed for the budget at all, and the loop bound is exact at any size. This is the second alternative the delta review gave for form 1.0.",
    "Sparse 0.0 (P1): the offset and numbytes records are no longer collected while the header is parsed; they are read from the block a second time when form 0.0 applies, and counted as they are converted. Nothing is collected before it is counted, which is what \"charge 0.0 ranges as they are collected\" asks for, by another route.",
    "The refusal stands where the oracle's own count passes the budget. A GNU.sparse.map is not split: it is read number by number, so an invalid number before that point is tarfile's ValueError as before, and one behind it is never looked at.",
    "The heap limit required, in the third fix round: 3,072 MiB, after 2,048 was measured to leave no margin under the budgets then proposed. Superseded by the closing step (`defaults`).",
    "Closing step, LOW-2 (the brief's point 2): the sparse budget is lowered to 2**16 and the integers of a global map are not shared. Lowering is the smaller and safer change: it touches no line of the reader that the delta review read, where sharing would add a cache that has to place the charge and a ValueError exactly where the oracle's walk puts them and would need mutants and a review of its own. Its price is that the cost of a wide map stays uncounted, bounded only through the count: about 115 MiB at 2**16, which is inside the measured peak (`defaults`). Sharing stays open as backlog B17 and comes first if the sparse budget is ever raised. The technical owner decided the same (\"Don't switch to shared BigInts\"), in a message that reached the implementer only after the commit; so this is her decision, and the reasons here are the implementer's own for having arrived at it.",
    "Closing step, pax records: 2**21 instead of a bound nothing reaches (`defaults`, `where_each_number_comes_from`). The vectors keep their three sides for every budget."
   ],
   "bounds": [
    "Four budgets, each a count over one listing (one tarfile.open and getmembers): metadataBytes 2**24 = 16,777,216; paxRecords 2**21 = 2,097,152; sparseNumbers 2**16 = 65,536; members 2**17 = 131,072. A listing that ends exactly at a budget is read; one unit more is refused. Nothing is credited back.",
    "The refusal is an OSError with the constant message \"tar metadata budget exceeded: \" followed by \"metadata bytes\", \"pax records\", \"sparse numbers\" or \"members\"; it holds no number and nothing from the archive. archive_manifest answers \"unreadable archive or manifest\", code E_ADAPTER_INPUT.",
    "D4 covers an archive in whose listing tarfile comes to pass one of the four counts, counted as test/differential/tar_budget.py counts them on the oracle's own tarfile, and nothing else. The port refuses at that point. What the oracle refuses before that point comes first in the port as well; what it answers from there on is replaced: acceptance, the same closed refusal (the public outcome does not change), or a class no handler of the engine names.",
    "D3 is a bound of its own on one block, asked before the byte budget is charged, and keeps its own refusal and vectors: a block above 2**28 bytes is refused under D3, whatever the budget. The byte budget is smaller, so a block between the two, one of exactly 2**28 included, is refused under D4 (metadata bytes), and archive_manifest never reads a block of the limit's size (ruling D3).",
    "The budgets are sized for a V8 heap limit (`heap_size_limit`) of at least 2,048 MiB (HEAP_LIMIT_REQUIRED_MIB). Under a lower limit a listing ends in UnsupportedRuntime before anything is parsed: a plain Error, not an archive refusal, without a public code (backlog B10). Measured at exactly that limit on the costliest archive found within the budgets: 321 to 341 MiB of heap in use at the peak of one listing, 631 to 801 MiB with the listing before it still referenced.",
    "What the heap requirement does not promise: the limit reserves no memory (a process is killed by its cgroup below it), and V8 passes it when the heap is nearly full; both were measured in the third fix round under the budgets then proposed and a limit of 3,072 MiB, and not again. It does not count Buffers or the compressed file (46 to 79 MiB of Buffers at the peak on the costliest archive, plus the archive). And what the caller holds on the heap comes out of the same limit: with 1,344 MiB of caller data Node 24.14.0 aborts on the costliest archive.",
    "What is shared between members, and why that is no copy: the integer of a size in a global header and the text of a name in a global header. Both are immutable values of the runtime; a member holds a reference, and a name with trailing slashes stripped is a view of the one text. test/unit/port-tar-budgets.test.js measures both: 20,000 members under a size of 4,300 digits, 2,000 members under a path of 4 MiB. A sparse list is not shared: every expansion makes its own and is counted. The size of its numbers is not counted either: a number of 4,300 digits is about 1.8 kB, and a map in a global header is converted anew at every expansion while its digits are charged as bytes once. At 2**16 numbers that is about 115 MiB no budget counts (the delta review of the third fix round, LOW-2; backlog B17).",
    "Not bounded by D4: time. An archive within all four budgets can take about an hour in the oracle and half an hour in the port (a name of 2**24 bytes in a global header, applied to every member); backlog B12."
