   "id": "C1",
   "subject": "precondition",
   "as_received": "Owner decision (Vasyl, poll 2296): finish this round with the conservative first-version contract already sent. One narrow delta review follows. New routes go to backlog, then task B and the catalog. Record in the rulings that the trusted-source, digest-checked-before-parse input is a caller precondition, to be confirmed at catalog integration, not a proven property. If F4 is left as residual risk of the trusted-input version, record it as that, changing the readiness criterion honestly, not as fixed or PASS.",
   "bounds": [
    "The input of the archive reader is taken to come from a trusted source and to be checked against its pinned digest before it is parsed. That is a precondition on the caller. It is to be confirmed at catalog integration and is not a proven property.",
    "What archive_manifest itself does: it compares the sha256 of the bytes it read with the digest its entry declares before the tar reader is called (archive.py:126-127, row 39). Where the entry and its digest come from, and whether that source is trusted, is the caller's.",
    "D4 is the first version of a contract for such input: limits with a clear margin above the package archives surveyed, a typed refusal above them, the supported corpus unchanged. It does not claim to bound what an archive built to be costly can do. Further amplification routes were not searched for after this decision; the ones known and not closed are in the backlog (B12, B13, B15).",
    "What D4 leaves of finding F4 is residual risk of this trusted-input version and is recorded as such under the finding. It is not recorded as fixed."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "test/port-rulings.golden (obligation archive.trusted-input, finding F4)",
    "docs/port-notes.md (\"The admission budgets\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "D5",
   "subject": "divergence",
   "closes": "plan question Q1 (approved plan 10.2) and backlog B24",
   "as_received": "The owner decided Q1 (Zulip #Tarn > kitchen, 2393, option C). Today the build's request id hashes the cook-adapter argv, i.e. the paths of Node and `dist/cook-adapter.js` (review L2: src/engine.ts:68-70, src/deploy.ts:582). That means every Node upgrade or move of kitchen rebuilds and restarts every agent. With C, kitchen's request identity uses a named, versioned adapter contract instead of paths, e.g. `kitchen-cook-adapter/1`. The number moves only when what the adapter puts into a build changes. The command actually executed stays the real one. Kitchen clone `.cache/kitchen/repo` is at 15ee4a1. This is a deliberate divergence from the oracle (which hashes `sys.executable` plus the script path). Record it as a named ruling, and make the corpus compare it on purpose rather than hide it: the oracle side keeps its own id, and the TS side must be shown to be identical in everything except the identity words. Add a guard so the version can't silently go stale: a test where the adapter's observable output (lock post-processing, modes, staged files) is pinned to the contract version, so any change in that output fails until the version is bumped.",
   "left_to_the_implementer": "The name of the contract and the shape of what is hashed. Chosen: the one word `kitchen-cook-adapter/1`, the example the brief gives, in the place of the two words `[interpreter, adapter script]`; every later word of the cook command is hashed as the oracle's shim words it. Also chosen: what the number covers (first bound below) and how the guard is built (third bound).",
   "bounds": [
    "The request id of a build is sha256 of the canonical `{\"spec\": ..., \"cook\": [...]}` in both engines (deploy.py:482; src/deploy.ts, the constructor). The oracle's `cook` is the cook command: its interpreter, the path of its adapter script, then `--spec {recipe} --out {build_dir} --state {state_dir} --runtime R --entrypoint E --bun-path B --node-path N`. A kitchen's `cook` is `kitchen-cook-adapter/<number>` and then the same fourteen words (src/engine.ts; ADAPTER_CONTRACT in src/cook.ts). Nothing else of the identity differs: the spec is the oracle's canonical request byte for byte, and the paths of cook's runtime, its entrypoint, Bun and Node stay in the name, since they go into the build.",
    "The command that is started is not changed: this Node and `dist/cook-adapter.js` of this install, with the same later words. It is no part of the name any more.",
    "The number is raised when, for some request, the adapter puts something else into a build: the recipe it writes or stages, the layout, `framework.lock.json`, the lock after its post-processing, a mode of one of those files, or the arguments and environment names it hands cook. It is raised for nothing else: not for another Node, another install location, a refactoring or a new refusal. test/corpus/adapter_contract.py holds that output on the public cases of the deploy corpus against a record kept under the name (test/corpus/adapter-contract/). It fails on any difference, refuses to rewrite a record for a change of the adapter, and keeps the digests of the cases and stand-ins so that a record can be replaced only when those changed. It sees what the three public cases exercise and no more: a change that shows on other input only is not caught by it and is the reviewer's to catch.",
    "`Deployment` takes the identity as an option of its own (`cook_identity`). Without it the command names the build, as in the oracle, and that is how the role's own call is still compared (`role` in a golden). Only `dispatch` of src/engine.ts gives it. `EngineOptions.identity` lets a caller hand in other words; the deploy corpus does so for one of its two runs and nothing else may.",
    "What it means for an agent the role deployed (finding 1 of the approved plan, unchanged by this ruling): the role's build has the role's name, which no kitchen gives. The first `apply` of a kitchen is therefore a new build beside it, one cook run and one restart of the unit, once for each agent, and `plan` says so beforehand. After that an upgrade of Node, a move of Node or a move of kitchen changes no name and causes no build and no restart. A change of the number does, once for each agent again.",
    "A build a kitchen made before this ruling has a name with the paths in it. One exists: the rehearsal build on the trial account (installed, never started). The next `apply` there is a new build as well."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "test/corpus/corpus.py compare: the section `build_name` of a golden (the oracle's adapter words and the words after them), `own_name_differences` (the TS engine with nobody naming the build for it: the oracle's record but for the adapter words and the digests that follow the name; the harness looks for the build under the oracle's formula with the contract word in the place of the shim's two), and the first run as before, where the engine is handed the oracle's words and every byte and digest is the oracle's",
    "test/corpus/corpus.py compare, `taking_over`: the role's apply through the Python engine, then a kitchen (another name, `plan` announces a build, one cook run with the adapter of this Node and install, one restart, then nothing), then the engine from another Node path and another install location (the same name, no cook run, no restart; a later release from there is started with that Node and that install)",
    "test/unit/corpus.test.js",
    "test/corpus/adapter_contract.py, test/corpus/adapter-contract/kitchen-cook-adapter-1.golden, test/unit/adapter-contract.test.js"
   ],
   "not_shown": "No real cook ran and no account was touched for this ruling. Cook is the stand-in or the replay of a kept build, as everywhere in the corpus.",
   "later": "Ruling D6 raised the number to 2 and withdrew the exception that had been made under `/1` for two environment names. This entry is kept as it was received."
  },
  {
   "id": "D6",
   "subject": "divergence",
   "closes": "finding M1 of the delta review of 40378ef..22caa8d",
   "as_received": "Vasyl's decision (poll 2535): bump the contract to `kitchen-cook-adapter/2`. The reviewer showed that a fresh per-request Bun cache can resolve unpinned ranges to newer versions. So \"the new names don't change what enters the build\" is false, and the `/1` exception goes. Record the `/2` contract and update the port notes and D5 text honestly: the fresh-cache acquisition is part of the contract. All four live agents will get a new build name and will rebuild once, in the later thin-Ansible window. That is accepted. Don't try to avoid it.",
   "left_to_the_implementer": "The name `kitchen-cook-adapter/2` is the brief's. Chosen by the implementer: that the record of `/2` holds the environment names of a cook call as they are handed over, with nothing held apart, and that the record of `/1` stays unchanged and unchecked. Also chosen, for the brief's point on local Git and so under the same number: that the adapter hands cook Git's counted configuration names (`GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`, `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_<n>`, `GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_<n>`) with one `url.file://PATH.insteadOf = PATH` for each source repository the recipe names by a local path, and none where there is no such repository; that `host_repo` is not rewritten; and that every `ROOT/.bun-cache-*` is removed before cook starts.",
   "bounds": [
    "A build named under `/2` was installed by cook with `BUN_INSTALL_CACHE_DIR` naming an empty directory of the build's own, selected by `COOK_PASS_ENV`, removed afterwards (src/cook.ts, src/isolation.ts). That acquisition is part of the contract. It can change the bytes of a build against one made with the account's cache: with nothing cached Bun asks the registry for every package and resolves a range no lockfile pins to the newest version that fits, where a warm cache can answer with an earlier one and no request (the review's probe_cache_resolution.py, real Bun 1.4.4 and a local registry).",
    "`/2` distinguishes the acquisition. It pins nothing: cook's install is not pinned beyond the two archives (backlog B26 in docs/port-notes.md), and two builds of one request under `/2` can differ.",
    "The exception made under `/1` is withdrawn. Every name in cook's environment is under the number. test/corpus/adapter_contract.py records them in full under `/2`; the comparison with the oracle still sets the names the port adds apart, by name, since the oracle's adapter has none of them (corpus.cook_calls).",
    "Git's configuration names are in cook's environment only where the recipe handed to cook names a source repository by a local path. A deployment whose sources are all remote has the environment names of `/1` and the two cache names, and no other.",
    "Every agent built under `/1` gets a new build name: one cook run and one restart each, once, in a later window. The brief accepts that and says not to avoid it. Nothing of it was done with this ruling.",
    "The refusal of a build that shares a file with another build is not weakened: it is the same check on the same files (src/isolation.ts `shared`)."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "src/cook.ts (ADAPTER_CONTRACT and the comment above it), src/isolation.ts",
    "test/corpus/adapter_contract.py, test/corpus/adapter-contract/kitchen-cook-adapter-2.golden, test/unit/adapter-contract.test.js",
    "test/unit/isolation.test.js: the sweep, the sources a recipe names, the configuration entries, and a real Git clone with them that shares no object file with its repository",
    "test/unit/test_takeover.py: a connector from a local repository the recipe names is built beside a build that has a linked clone of it; one the recipe does not name is still refused; a cache a killed run left is gone after the next prepare",
    "test/corpus/corpus.py compare on the public and on the private cases, Node 22 and 24: each line names the build by `kitchen-cook-adapter/2`"
   ],
   "not_shown": "No real cook ran for this ruling and no account was touched: cook is the stand-in or the replay of a kept build. That the pinned cook hands `GIT_CONFIG_*` on to its Git was read in its source (`src/process-env.ts`, `gitEnvironment`) and its function was called with a real Git; cook's install was not run with a local connector. That Git applies `insteadOf` to a path given to `clone` was seen with Git 2.43.0 only. The Git of no live host was asked. Which package versions a rebuild of a live agent under `/2` resolves was not looked at."
  }
 ],
 "obligations": [
  {
   "id": "archive.L140.4",
   "ruling": "M1",
   "status": "discharged",
   "owed": "An end-to-end vector, through cook.main or the deployment boundary, on which the mutant gives another public code than the original.",
   "due_when_any_exists": [
    "src/cook.ts",
    "src/deploy.ts"
   ],
   "discharged_with": {
    "test": "test/unit/engine.test.js",
    "name": "archive.L140.4 end to end: at the deployment boundary the mutant gives another public code than the original, in both engines",
    "what_it_shows": "A package manifest that is a JSON list, in a host dependency of a contract v1 plan. Original, oracle and port: E_ADAPTER_INPUT, phase prepare. Mutant (archive.py:140 and the TS mutant of test/mutants/ts-anchors.golden), oracle and port: E_DEPLOY_FAILED. The test asks all four and writes no code down."
   },
   "discharged_by": "Entered by the implementer together with src/deploy.ts and src/cook.ts, which made it due. No review has read the witness yet; it is part of the slice whose review is owed (scripts/check-port-ready blocks on those commits)."
  },
  {
   "id": "archive.trusted-input",
