    "src/catalog.ts"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "open_findings": [
  {
   "id": "F1",
   "found": "by the implementer in the fix round, while measuring what the archive reader still holds in memory",
   "status": "ruled",
   "finding": "A tar name or a pax value longer than 536,870,888 UTF-16 units (the longest string of the JavaScript runtime) ends the port in a RangeError of the runtime, where the oracle accepts the archive. Measured with a pax value and a GNU long name of 600 MiB: the oracle accepts at 2.4 GiB and 1.8 GiB peak, the port fails closed at 1.2 GiB. At 300 MiB both accept. Present at ac5e89c as well; the fix round did not change it.",
   "asks": "Ruled in the second fix round: a declared refusal, divergence D3.",
   "backlog": "B8",
   "ruling": "D3"
  },
  {
   "id": "F4",
   "found": "by the implementer in the second fix round, while checking that the limit of D3 is enough on its own",
   "status": "ruled",
   "finding": "Valid archives at or below the metadata limit of D3 on which the oracle answers and the port does not: the port runs out of heap and the process aborts (\"JavaScript heap out of memory\", exit by SIGABRT, no receipt). The heap limit is the runtime's, about 4 GiB by default on Node 22.23.3 and 24.14.0, less on a small machine, more with --max-old-space-size; it can come before the memory of the machine does. The oracle has no such ceiling: it ends in an uncaught MemoryError only when the machine has no more. It fails closed. No limit on a single block removes it.",
   "measured": [
    "One pax header of 268,435,417 bytes (below the limit) that holds one GNU.sparse.map of 2**27 numbers: the oracle accepts in 9 s at 7.6 GiB peak; the port aborts after 29 s at 5.7 GiB resident.",
    "Sixteen members, each with a GNU long name of exactly 2**28 bytes (4 GiB of names): the oracle accepts in 5 s at 4.6 GiB peak; the port aborts after 31 s at 4.4 GiB resident. With eight such members both accept (oracle 2.6 GiB, port 4.2 GiB).",
    "Read by both engines at the limit: 53.6 million pax records of five bytes (oracle 27 s, port 7 s); 2**24 + 4,096 distinct pax keywords (oracle 8 s at 2.1 GiB, port 3 s at 0.3 GiB, after the fix F3); 11.6 million records of the sparse form 0.0 (oracle 7 s, port 4 s).",
    "In the delta review of the second fix round (P1): a GNU.sparse.map of 2**20 numbers in a global header and 80 members behind one-record extended headers, 3.3 KB compressed: the oracle accepts at 2.9 GiB; the port aborts at 4.1 GiB on Node 22.23.3 and 4.0 GiB on 24.14.0. With D4 the same archive is refused at the second expansion of the map, in 0.2 s, at 173 MiB resident on Node 22.23.3 and 153 MiB on 24.14.0."
   ],
   "asks": "Ruled in direction, twice. While the second fix round was being closed: keep it open and red, with the measurements and a written proposal, and build nothing then (the lines are quoted under ruling D4, `earlier_lines_of_the_technical_owner`). In the brief of the third fix round: \"build F4 as declared divergence D4\", with numbers \"calibrated from evidence, not asserted\", and \"F4 and port-ready stay red until a delta review of this round.\" By the owner's decision during that round (ruling C1) the round is finished as a conservative first version for trusted input, and what it leaves is recorded as residual risk. What is still asked: approval of the proposed defaults of ruling D4, and the one narrow delta review of the third fix round. Both were given by the closing step: the defaults are approved (ruling D4, `defaults`) and that review is recorded (`delta_review`). Nothing further is asked for the success path; hardening is later work by the owner's direction.",
   "backlog": "B11",
   "ruling": "D4",
   "residual_risk": [
    "The limits are approved for the success path on trusted input, not as a bound on what an archive built to be costly can do. The memory they need is a requirement on an environment that does not exist yet.",
    "The costliest archive within the budgets is the costliest that was built, not a proven maximum. No further amplification routes were searched for after the owner's decision in the third fix round.",
    "The sparse budget counts numbers and not their size (the delta review of the third fix round, LOW-2; backlog B17). It is not fixed in the reader. At 2**16 numbers the uncounted cost of a wide global map is about 115 MiB, and it is inside the measured peak. If the sparse budget is raised without the fix, the margin of the heap requirement no longer holds.",
    "The V8 heap limit is not a hard bound. With more of the caller's data on the heap than was measured to fit (1,076 MiB inside the limit on both runtimes), a listing within the budgets can still end the process: with 1,344 MiB Node 24.14.0 aborted.",
    "In a unit of less than 4 GiB of memory Node's default heap is below the requirement, and the reader refuses every archive with UnsupportedRuntime until Node is started with the flag (backlog B14). It fails closed and loudly.",
    "Time is not bounded (backlog B12): an archive within every budget can take about an hour in the oracle and half an hour in the port.",
    "All of this is accepted for input that is trusted and digest-checked before it is parsed. That is a precondition on the caller (ruling C1, obligation archive.trusted-input), to be confirmed at catalog integration."
   ],
   "ruled_not_fixed": "Ruled as divergence D4 with the residual risk below, which is accepted for trusted input (ruling C1). It is not fixed and not closed: scripts/check-port-ready prints it as residual risk, and the precondition of C1 comes due with the catalog (obligation archive.trusted-input). Set in the closing step on the manager's answer quoted under `delta_review.set_by`.",
   "residual_risk_accepted_by": "C1"
  }
 ],
 "backlog": [
  {
   "id": "B1",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse a recipe that is not UTF-8 in `verify_bytes`, in both engines, by a reviewed waiver."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2",
   "for": "oracle and plan",
   "entry": "Bound document depth explicitly, well below the interpreter limits, so that no refusal depends on the caller's depth."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3",
   "for": "plan",
   "entry": "Pin the oracle interpreter by the sha256 of its files (urllib/parse.py, tarfile.py, gzip.py, json/decoder.py), not by the name \"CPython 3.12.3\"."
  },
  {
   "id": "B4",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse an archive whose tar listing ends on an invalid header instead of an end marker."
  },
  {
   "id": "B5",
   "for": "port, when cook and catalog are ported",
   "entry": "Decide whether the key order of the merged configuration is observable downstream. If it is, port the order rule of `merge_hash` (an equal upper mapping replaces the lower one)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B6",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "`combine` in src/config.ts is the stand-in and matches Ansible only in the `merge_layers` shape. A second caller needs Ansible's semantics for three or more layers and for layers that are not mappings."
  },
  {
   "id": "B7",
   "for": "port, with cook or deploy",
   "entry": "The end-to-end witness for archive.L140.4 (obligation above)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B8",
   "for": "closed",
   "entry": "Open finding F1, ruled as divergence D3 in the second fix round."
  },
  {
   "id": "B9",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "The stepwise gzip reader drives Node's zlib engine through the call `inflateRawSync` uses internally, on the Node versions of STEPWISE_RUNTIMES only (ruling B9). Replace it when Node documents a synchronous inflate that can be resumed. Until then two modes of a changed engine end as a crash and not as `UnsupportedRuntime` (review finding L2): an engine whose errors arrive only asynchronously leaves an unhandled `error` event, and a change the checks of the reader do not name surfaces as whatever the runtime throws."
  },
  {
   "id": "B10",
   "for": "port, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Check the runtime once at start and report an unverified one as a missing prerequisite with a public code, before any archive is read. Today it surfaces at the first archive, as `UnsupportedRuntime` without a code."
  },
  {
   "id": "B11",
   "for": "review",
   "entry": "Finding F4, built as divergence D4 in the third fix round. Its two debts of that round are paid: the defaults are approved (ruling D4) and the round's delta review is recorded as passed. The finding is ruled with residual risk, not fixed. Owed still: a review of the closing step's own commit, on which scripts/check-port-ready blocks; how that line is cleared is for the manager and the technical owner to settle."
  },
  {
   "id": "B12",
   "for": "ruling",
   "entry": "Open; nothing built; needs a ruling. Time is not bounded by D4. A name value in a global pax header is applied to every later member, and both engines look at the whole name for each member (the oracle in `rstrip` and `re.fullmatch`, the port in `rstripSlash` and the manifest-name test). The byte budget of 2**24 bounds the name, so the cost fell sixteenfold with it. Measured in the closing step through archive_manifest with one path of 2**24 bytes in a global header, 100 and 500 members, on a shared host: for a path that is a run of slashes 0.013 to 0.016 s a member in the port and 0.031 s in the oracle; for one without a trailing slash 0.0002 s in the port and 0.0065 s in the oracle. At the member budget that is about half an hour in the port and a little over an hour in the oracle, on an archive built for it; a real package archive has no global name at all. One way to bound it: count a name inherited from a global header against the byte budget each time it is applied, as a global sparse map is counted each time it is expanded. Does not block use on trusted input."
  },
  {
   "id": "B13",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "Open, low value now. `decodeUtf8` builds a long text in pieces and the first use flattens it, so a metadata block costs twice its text at the peak. Under the byte budget of 2**24 that is at most about 32 MiB of a peak of some 330 MiB (it was 0.5 GiB under 2**28); the heap requirement was sized with the cost as it is."
  },
  {
   "id": "B14",
   "for": "role, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Open for units of less than 4 GiB; nothing is needed above that. The reader requires a V8 heap limit of at least 2,048 MiB. Node's default meets it where the unit has 4 GiB of memory or more: measured with no flag in scopes of MemoryMax 4G, 6G and 8G, where the default limit is 2,096 MiB on Node 22.23.3 and 2,240 MiB on 24.14.0 and the costliest archive is read. Under MemoryMax=2G the default is 1,048 or 1,120 MiB and the reader refuses every archive with `UnsupportedRuntime` and no public code (B10), until whoever starts kitchen passes `--max-old-space-size=2048` on the command line of Node (the role refuses NODE_OPTIONS in a unit environment). No launcher or unit for kitchen exists yet, and the role was not changed. Not built: the obligation the delta review suggested, which would turn the readiness check red when a launcher for kitchen exists without the flag; where kitchen is to run in less than 4 GiB, add it with the launcher."
  },
  {
   "id": "B15",
   "for": "ruling, at catalog integration",
   "entry": "Known and not closed, by the owner's decision for the trusted-input version (ruling C1): the compressed archive is held as a whole, in both engines, and its size is bounded by nothing here; what the caller holds on the heap comes out of the limit the reader requires; and no further amplification routes were searched for. If the precondition of C1 is dropped, these are the first to look at, with B12."
  },
  {
   "id": "B16",
   "for": "readiness check",
   "entry": "Open; not fixed; reproduced by the reviewer (the delta review of the third fix round, LOW-1). scripts/check-port-ready misses a source file that is moved under docs/: `git log --name-only` shows a rename by its new name only, and the porcelain line of a rename is cut to the part after \" -> \", so a commit or an uncommitted change that moves a source file under docs/ counts as notes. A plain deletion does block. The fix the reviewer names: pass `--no-renames` to both `git log` and `git status`, with a case in the readiness unit test. Left for the backlog by the brief of the closing step. Until it is fixed, a reader of a PASS should look at the NOTE lines, which still name every later commit."
  },
  {
   "id": "B17",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "Open; not fixed in the reader; bounded by a default instead (the delta review of the third fix round, LOW-2). The sparse budget counts numbers and not their size. A GNU.sparse.map in a global header is converted into fresh integers at every expansion; a number of 4,300 digits is about 1.8 kB, and its digits are charged as metadata bytes once, with the global header. Under the budget of 2**20 then proposed, the reviewer's archive kept 1,907 MiB that no budget counted and aborted Node 24.14.0 at the heap limit then required with 524 MiB of caller data. The closing step lowers the sparse budget to 2**16, which bounds that cost at about 115 MiB; the shape is part of combined.heap-worst in test/differential/port_budget.py and of the measurements behind the heap requirement. The fix: convert a map that comes from a global header once and share the immutable integers between expansions, as `converted` does for a global size, recording where a conversion fails so that the charge and the ValueError fall where the oracle's walk puts them; or charge the map's bytes at every expansion, which adds refusals and vectors. Do this before raising the sparse budget."
  },
  {
   "id": "B18",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "Open, entered by the implementer with the success-path port of deploy and cook: `catalog.ts`, and with it the catalog branches of `deploy.ts` and `cook.ts`. Until then an active catalog is refused. None of the five deployments activates it"
  },
  {
   "id": "B19",
   "for": "port, before any use on input that is not ours",
   "entry": "Open, entered by the implementer with the success-path port of deploy and cook: `deploy.ts` and `cook.ts` have no mutants and no hostile vectors; the inventory rows they own (plan section 4.1) are not marked; exception classes on wrongly typed values are not modelled throughout. They are not listed as ported"
  },
  {
   "id": "B20",
   "for": "whoever allows it (plan step 5)",
   "entry": "Open, entered by the implementer with the success-path port of deploy and cook: `verify_cook` has met no real cook checkout, and cook has not run under kitchen. The lock half is done (plan step 4): the lock of one live build is replayed in a case outside the repository, and both engines take it alike"
  },
  {
   "id": "B21",
   "for": "corpus",
   "entry": "Open, entered by the implementer with the success-path port of deploy and cook: cases for the branches no case reaches: `systemd-analyze` under `install_only` and `E_UNIT_ACTIVE`, rollback, a retired unit, a dropped secret file, a health command, an engine-rendered unit, connectors from descriptors, the ownership refusals"
  },
  {
   "id": "B22",
   "for": "port, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Open, entered by the implementer with the success-path port of deploy and cook: there is no operational entry: `src/cli.ts` refuses. `src/engine.ts` is a library call; secrets on a descriptor, the receipt file and exit statuses are not built. A rehearsal needs a runner"
  },
